Welcome to Week 2 of our training. As a developer with 5 years of experience, you already know *how* to use these tools, but this week is about understanding *why* they work the way they do at the architectural level. We are going to strip these concepts down to their simplest beginner analogies, and then build them back up with the rigorous, low-level JVM mechanics that tech interviewers look for in a mid-senior engineer.
Here is your in-depth theory, paired with real-world interview questions and coding exercises derived from standard 5-year Java developer interview guides.
### **1. Matrix & 2D Array Structures**
**The Beginner Analogy:** Think of a 2D array like a spreadsheet with rows and columns. It’s just a grid where you put data into specific cells, like grid[row][column].
**The 5-Year Developer Deep Dive:** Java does not have true 2D arrays in the way C or C++ does (which allocate one massive contiguous block of memory). Instead, Java uses "arrays of arrays."
When you declare int[][] matrix = new int[3][4], the JVM creates one array of size 3 in the Heap memory. This array doesn't hold integers; it holds *references* (pointers) to three separate arrays of size 4.
Because of this, Java easily supports "jagged arrays" (e.g., row 0 has 5 columns, but row 1 has 2 columns). In-place matrix operations (like rotating a matrix 90°) are a massive signal of a developer's spatial reasoning and pointer management skills, as they require O(1) auxiliary space.
**Basic Coding Question:** Iterate the primary diagonal of an N \times N matrix.
 * *Code:* ```java
   for (int i = 0; i < matrix.length; i++) {
   System.out.println(matrix[i][i]);
   }
   ```
   
   ```
 * *Explanation:* The primary diagonal always exists where the row and column indices match. Using a single loop instead of nested loops optimizes time complexity from O(N^2) to O(N).
**Advanced Coding / 5-Year Interview Question:** Rotate an N \times N 2D matrix by 90 degrees clockwise *in-place*.
 * *Code:*
   ```java
   // 1. Transpose the matrix (swap matrix[i][j] with matrix[j][i])
   for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
       for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
           int temp = matrix[i][j];
           matrix[i][j] = matrix[j][i];
           matrix[j][i] = temp;
       }
   }
   // 2. Reverse each row
   for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
       for (int j = 0; j < n / 2; j++) {
           int temp = matrix[i][j];
           matrix[i][j] = matrix[i][n - 1 - j];
           matrix[i][n - 1 - j] = temp;
       }
   }
   
   ```
 * *Explanation:* Interviewers ask this to see if you can manage multi-step algorithm transformations without relying on a temporary new int[n][n] grid, which would cost O(N^2) space. Transposing and then reversing is the mathematically optimal O(1) space solution.
### **2. Two-Pointer Algorithmic Frameworks**
**The Beginner Analogy:** Imagine you are looking for a specific word in a dictionary. Instead of reading every word one by one, you place your left finger at the beginning of the book and your right finger at the end, and move them closer to the middle based on alphabetical order.
**The 5-Year Developer Deep Dive:** The Two-Pointer technique is a memory optimization framework designed to reduce nested loops (reducing O(N^2) to O(N)).
 * **Opposite-Directional Pointers:** Typically used on *sorted* arrays. You evaluate the sum of the two pointers. If it's too high, you decrement the right pointer. If it's too low, you increment the left.
 * **Same-Directional Pointers (Fast/Slow):** Often known as Floyd’s Cycle Detection algorithm. A slow pointer moves one step, a fast pointer moves two. This is critical for array partitioning or detecting loops in linked lists without using extra memory (like a HashSet).
**Basic Coding Question:** Move all zeroes in an array to the end while maintaining the relative order of non-zero elements.
 * *Code:*
   ```java
   int slow = 0;
   for (int fast = 0; fast < nums.length; fast++) {
       if (nums[fast] != 0) {
           int temp = nums[slow];
           nums[slow++] = nums[fast];
           nums[fast] = temp;
       }
   }
   
   ```
 * *Explanation:* The fast pointer scans the array. The slow pointer marks the boundary of where the next non-zero number belongs. When fast finds a non-zero, they swap, pushing zeroes back automatically.
**Advanced Coding / 5-Year Interview Question:** Find if a linked list has a cycle.
 * *Explanation:* Initialize ListNode slow = head, fast = head. Loop while fast != null && fast.next != null. Move slow by 1 and fast by 2. If slow == fast, a cycle exists. It proves your understanding of in-place cycle detection without relying on O(N) hash-based memory.
### **3. Sliding Window Techniques**
**The Beginner Analogy:** Imagine looking through a small magnifying glass at a long line of numbers. The magnifying glass can only see 3 numbers at a time. To see the next group, you just slide the glass one step to the right.
**The 5-Year Developer Deep Dive:** Sliding Window is an advanced subset of the two-pointer framework used exclusively for tracking *contiguous* subarray metrics.
Instead of recalculating a sum for every subset (which takes O(N \times K) time), you calculate the first window, and as you "slide" it forward, you simply add the new element entering the right side of the window, and subtract the old element falling off the left side.
 * **Fixed-length:** The window size (K) never changes.
 * **Variable-length:** The right pointer expands to ingest data until a condition breaks. Then, the left pointer shrinks the window until the condition is valid again.
**Basic Coding Question:** Find the maximum sum of any contiguous subarray of size K.
 * *Code:*
   ```java
   int windowSum = 0, maxSum = 0;
   for(int i = 0; i < k; i++) windowSum += arr[i]; // Initial window
   maxSum = windowSum;
   
   for(int i = k; i < arr.length; i++) {
       windowSum += arr[i] - arr[i - k]; // Slide window
       maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, windowSum);
   }
   
   ```
 * *Explanation:* By adding the incoming element arr[i] and subtracting the outgoing element arr[i-k], the time complexity collapses to O(N).
**Advanced Coding / 5-Year Interview Question:** Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters (Variable Window).
 * *Explanation:* Use a HashSet to store characters in the current window. Expand the right pointer. If right sees a duplicate, enter a while loop to increment left and remove characters from the set until the duplicate is evicted. Keep track of the Math.max(maxLength, right - left + 1).
### **4. Bitwise Operations & Masks**
**The Beginner Analogy:** Computers don't actually understand numbers like 5 or 10; they only understand switches turned ON (1) or OFF (0). Bitwise operations let you reach inside the computer and flip those exact switches manually.
**The 5-Year Developer Deep Dive:** Operating directly on the CPU registry using bitwise logic (&, |, ^, <<, >>) is the most computationally efficient way to process data in Java.
You use "Masks" (a specific binary pattern) to isolate bits. For example, applying a bitwise AND (&) with a mask of 1 instantly tells you if a number is odd or even (parity checking), completely bypassing the much slower modulo (%) operator. The expression x & (-x) is a famous bitwise trick to isolate the rightmost set bit, leveraging Two's Complement arithmetic.
**Basic Coding Question:** Check if a number is even without using %.
 * *Code:* boolean isEven = (n & 1) == 0;
 * *Explanation:* In binary, all odd numbers end in 1. The mask 1 (which is 000...001) zeroes out every other bit. If the result is 0, the last bit was 0, meaning it is even.
**Advanced Coding / 5-Year Interview Question:** You have an array where every number appears twice, except for one number. Find that single number in O(N) time and O(1) space.
 * *Code:*
   ```java
   int result = 0;
   for (int num : nums) {
       result ^= num; // XOR operation
   }
   return result;
   
   ```
 * *Explanation:* This tests your knowledge of the XOR (^) operator. XORing two identical numbers results in 0 (they cancel out). XORing a number with 0 returns the number. Therefore, XORing everything in the array will cancel out all pairs, leaving only the unique number in the result variable.
### **5. Hashing Mechanics & Hash Contracts**
**The Beginner Analogy:** Imagine a coat check room at a hotel. You hand over your coat (the Value), and they give you a unique ticket with a number on it (the Hash). When you return, they don't search through every coat; they just look at your ticket number and go straight to that exact hook.
**The 5-Year Developer Deep Dive:** This is the most critical topic for a mid-level Java interview. A java.util.HashMap is backed by an array of "buckets." When you call put(Key, Value), Java runs the Key's hashCode() to calculate an integer, and uses modulo math to determine which bucket index to place the entry in.
 * **The Contract:** If two objects are logically equal (equals() returns true), their hashCode() MUST return the same integer. If you override one, you must override both.
 * **Collisions & Java 8 Changes:** If two different keys generate the same bucket index (a collision), Java forms a LinkedList inside that bucket (Separate Chaining). In Java 8, if this linked list grows to 8 or more elements (and the total capacity is \ge 64), the JVM automatically converts the linked list into a Red-Black Tree. This safeguards performance, preventing worst-case O(N) lookups and guaranteeing O(\log N) time.
 * **Load Factor:** By default, it is 0.75. When the map becomes 75% full, it automatically resizes (doubles the bucket array) and recalculates the hashes for everything (rehashing) to spread them out.
**Basic Coding Question:** Write a custom class to be used as a key in a HashMap.
 * *Code:*
   ```java
   class EmployeeKey {
       int id;
       String department;
   
       @Override
       public boolean equals(Object o) {
           if (this == o) return true;
           if (o == null || getClass() != o.getClass()) return false;
           EmployeeKey that = (EmployeeKey) o;
           return id == that.id && department.equals(that.department);
       }
   
       @Override
       public int hashCode() {
           return Objects.hash(id, department);
       }
   }
   
   ```
**Advanced 5-Year Interview Question:** "What happens if you use a mutable object as a HashMap key, put it in the map, and then modify the object's fields?"
 * *Answer:* The map will effectively "lose" the object. When you modify the fields, the object's hashCode() output changes. However, the HashMap doesn't know this; it already stored the object in a bucket based on the *old* hash code. When you later call map.get(key), Java calculates the *new* hash code, looks in the wrong bucket, and returns null. This is why immutable classes (like String and Integer) are the standard for HashMap keys.


